---
import PlaygroundShell from '../../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Lose the leader" hint="The machine that leads a partition is lost while its followers are a little behind. Choose when the producer is told a write is safe, how many copies exist, how many machines die and how far behind the followers are, and see which records are gone. A simple model with round numbers, not LinkedIn's or Kafka's code.">
  <ol class="how">
    <li>Press <b>Reset</b>. With <b>Leader only</b> acknowledgement, <b>3</b> copies, <b>1</b> machine lost, followers <b>200</b> ms behind and <b>100</b> thousand records per second, <b>20,000</b> records that the producer was told were safe are gone, and <b>0</b> consumers' reads are lost.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Producer is told a write is safe</b> to <b>All in-sync copies have it</b>: <b>0</b> acknowledged records are lost, and <b>20,000</b> records are ones the producer was never told were safe. The 2014 benchmark figure falls from <b>786,980</b> to <b>421,823</b> records per second.</li>
    <li>Press <b>Reset</b> (so it is <b>Leader only</b> again), then set the lag to <b>0</b> ms: <b>0</b> records are lost, because the followers have everything.</li>
    <li>Press <b>Reset</b>, then set <b>Machines lost at once</b> to <b>3</b>: every copy is gone, and no acknowledgement setting helps.</li>
    <li>Press <b>Reset</b>, then set the rate to <b>400</b> thousand and the lag to <b>500</b> ms: <b>200,000</b> acknowledged records are lost.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="pg kfk" data-kfk>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <label>Producer is told a write is safe <select class="pg-in" data-ack><option value="leader">Leader only (after its own write)</option><option value="all">All in-sync copies have it</option></select></label>
      <label>Copies of the partition <input type="range" min="1" max="3" value="3" data-c /> <b data-cval></b></label>
      <label>Machines lost at once, including the leader's <input type="range" min="1" max="3" value="1" data-m /> <b data-mval></b></label>
      <label>Followers are behind the leader by (ms) <input type="range" min="0" max="1000" step="50" value="200" data-l /> <b data-lval></b></label>
      <label>Records written per second (thousands) <input type="range" min="10" max="800" step="10" value="100" data-r /> <b data-rval></b></label>
      <button type="button" class="btn" data-reset>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-grid">
      <div class="pg-box" data-b1><h3>Acknowledged records that are lost</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o1></span><span class="pg-note" data-n1></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b2><h3>Records the producer was never told were safe</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o2></span><span class="pg-note" data-n2></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b3><h3>Records a consumer had already read that are lost</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o3></span><span class="pg-note" data-n3></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b4><h3>Throughput in LinkedIn's 2014 benchmark</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o4></span><span class="pg-note" data-n4></span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="pg-out" data-verdict aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p class="pg-note">Model, invented for teaching and not LinkedIn data. The leader's machine is lost for good, with its disk, at the moment the followers are the set number of milliseconds behind. Every follower is assumed to be behind by the same amount, so the records in that gap are the rate times the lag. With acknowledgement after the leader's own write, the producer has been told those records are safe, and they are lost if the leader was the only holder. With acknowledgement after all in-sync copies, the producer was not told, so it knows to resend them. Consumers only read records that every in-sync copy has, as the 2014 post says, so a lost leader does not take back records a consumer has seen, as long as one copy survives. If the machines lost are at least as many as the copies, nothing in the model survives, and it cannot say how much was lost. The benchmark numbers are from the post: 821,557 records per second with no replication, 786,980 with three copies and leader-only acknowledgement, 421,823 with three copies and all in-sync copies, all from one producer thread, on default settings with 100-byte records. The post did not measure two copies.</p>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-kfk]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = <T extends HTMLElement>(s: string) => root.querySelector<T>(s)!;
    const input = (k: string) => q<HTMLInputElement>(`[data-${k}]`);
    const select = (k: string) => q<HTMLSelectElement>(`[data-${k}]`);
    const DEFAULTS: Record<string, string> = { ack: 'leader', c: '3', m: '1', l: '200', r: '100' };
    const BENCH = { none: 821557, leader: 786980, all: 421823 }; // records per second, 2014 post, one producer thread
    const fmt = (n: number) => Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-US');

    function draw() {
      const ack = select('ack').value; const copies = Number(input('c').value); const lost = Number(input('m').value);
      const lagMs = Number(input('l').value); const rateK = Number(input('r').value);
      ['c', 'm', 'l', 'r'].forEach((k) => (q(`[data-${k}val]`).textContent = input(k).value));
      select('ack').disabled = copies === 1;
      const gap = (rateK * 1000 * lagMs) / 1000; // records the followers do not have yet
      const allGone = lost >= copies;
      const ackLost = !allGone && ack === 'leader' && copies > 1 ? gap : 0;
      const unsafe = !allGone && ack === 'all' && copies > 1 ? gap : 0;
      q('[data-o1]').textContent = allGone ? 'everything held only here' : fmt(ackLost);
      q('[data-n1]').textContent = allGone ? 'no copy survives, so no setting can promise anything' : ackLost > 0 ? 'the producer was told these were safe' : 'every acknowledged record survives on another copy';
      q('[data-o2]').textContent = allGone ? 'not applicable' : fmt(unsafe);
      q('[data-n2]').textContent = unsafe > 0 ? 'the producer knows these are not confirmed, and can resend them' : 'none: all records were already confirmed or already on every copy';
      q('[data-o3]').textContent = allGone ? 'cannot be promised' : '0';
      q('[data-n3]').textContent = allGone ? 'no copy of the log is left' : 'consumers only read records every in-sync copy has';
      const bench = copies === 1 ? BENCH.none : copies === 3 ? BENCH[ack === 'all' ? 'all' : 'leader'] : 0;
      q('[data-o4]').textContent = bench ? `${fmt(bench)} records per second` : 'not measured';
      q('[data-n4]').textContent = copies === 2 ? 'the post tested one and three copies only' : copies === 1 ? 'no replication, so the acknowledgement setting does not apply' : 'one producer thread, 100-byte records, default settings';
      const bad = [allGone || ackLost > 0, false, allGone, false];
      ['b1', 'b2', 'b3', 'b4'].forEach((b, i) => q<HTMLElement>(`[data-${b}]`).style.setProperty('--c', bad[i] ? 'var(--bad)' : 'var(--good)'));
      const out = q('[data-verdict]'); const failed = allGone || ackLost > 0; out.classList.toggle('bad', failed);
      if (allGone) out.textContent = `All ${copies} cop${copies === 1 ? 'y was' : 'ies were'} on lost machines, so the partition's data is gone. Copies on separate machines only help if fewer machines fail than there are copies.`;
      else if (ackLost > 0) out.textContent = `${fmt(ackLost)} records were acknowledged but only the lost leader had them. Wait for all in-sync copies if that loss matters.`;
      else if (unsafe > 0) out.textContent = `Nothing acknowledged was lost. ${fmt(unsafe)} records were never confirmed, so the producer resends them. The price is lower throughput.`;
      else out.textContent = 'Nothing acknowledged was lost: the surviving copies already had every record.';
    }
    root.addEventListener('input', draw);
    root.addEventListener('change', draw);
    q('[data-reset]').addEventListener('click', () => {
      select('ack').value = DEFAULTS.ack;
      ['c', 'm', 'l', 'r'].forEach((k) => (input(k).value = DEFAULTS[k]));
      draw();
    });
    draw();
  });
</script>
